# Build context exclusions.
#
# This file is a SECURITY control as much as a speed one. Anything listed here
# cannot reach a build layer, so it cannot reach `docker history`, a leaked
# image, or a public registry. The repository is public (AGENTS.md), but the
# untracked files beside it are not.

# --- secrets. Never in the context, never in a layer. Real values arrive at
# runtime from the host's secret store only. `.env.example` holds names with
# empty values and is still excluded: the image has no use for it.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# --- local provider evidence and generated reports. `evidence/raw/` and
# `reports/` are git-ignored, so they exist only on the owner's machine and
# would otherwise be silently baked into the image.
evidence/
reports/

# --- one-off investigation scripts, never committed and never shipped.
scripts/scratch/

# --- git history. A full .git in the context is both large and a disclosure
# risk (it carries every branch and every past state).
.git/
.gitattributes

# --- anything the build regenerates. Copying a host-built node_modules or
# .next into the context is how a Windows-built, non-standalone bundle ends up
# in a Linux image.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.next/
**/.next/
dist/
**/dist/
coverage/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# --- test and tooling artefacts with no role in a production image.
playwright-report/
**/playwright-report/
test-results/
**/test-results/
*.log

# --- developer and CI files the image does not need.
.github/
.husky/
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.jobs
.dockerignore
.DS_Store
